-- rip.rofl — RIP: Rest In Peace. Why tasks die in a dead letter queue.
--
-- A DLQ is a graveyard, and the tool answers the question nobody asks over
-- it: why did these tasks end up here, and which ones will be next. The
-- domain is a workflow with unreliable external calls and non-idempotent
-- stages — order, reserve, charge, ship, with compensation — against an
-- environment that can succeed, fail, time out, go quiet, or fail AFTER the
-- effect has already landed.
--
-- WHY THIS EXAMPLE EXISTS, and it is one property and not three.
-- Every other example in this repository accumulates facts monotonically.
-- This one alternates quantifiers: a state settles if there EXISTS an action
-- such that ALL of the environment's answers lead to states that settle. The
-- universal quantifier is what makes this an AND/OR graph, and it is written
-- the only way a rule language can write a universal over an open set — as
-- the negation of an existential:
--
--     will_settle(S) :- action(S, A), not risky(S, A).
--     risky(S, A)    :- respond(S, A, _, S2), not will_settle(S2).
--
-- `will_settle` depends on itself through two negations. That is a negative
-- cycle, and the ordinary phase-ordered evaluator is RIGHT to refuse it: the
-- number the stratum table computes does not exist inside such a cycle.
-- `semantics(well_founded).` swaps the phase ordering for an alternating
-- fixpoint, and the states the two limits disagree about come back as
-- `unknown(will_settle(S))` — which is this example's product.
--
-- THE THIRD CATEGORY IS NOT A THIRD VALUE SOMEBODY ASSERTED.
--
--   settles       there is a strategy: whatever the providers do, the task
--                 comes to rest with the books balanced
--   doomed        the environment can FORCE the books not to balance. This
--                 is a bug to fix, not a task to retry
--   dead letter   neither is derivable. The play can go on for ever, so no
--                 strategy guarantees the good end and no adversary
--                 guarantees the bad one. It depends on luck
--
-- The third is the COMPLEMENT of the fixpoint, and the kernel computes it as
-- one: an atom reaches the gap only by being DERIVED in a generous round, so
-- it carries the firing that derived it and `why unknown(will_settle(S))`
-- walks the circular dependency and names the unfounded set. It is not
-- "everything left over" — a store where nothing is unknown and a store
-- where everything is would both prove nothing, and §5 of demo.ts pins the
-- undefined set as a PROPER, NON-EMPTY part of the state space from both
-- sides: delete the carrier's silence and it is empty, restore it and it is
-- exactly twenty-four states.
--
-- And it is not the budget either. A run cut short reports
-- `hole(Id, budget_exhausted)` and claims no unknowns at all; demo.ts §5
-- shows the same program answering both ways at two budgets.
--
-- THE PERSPECTIVE IS A LEDGER, and there are two books here.
-- `[main]` is the analysis: the machine, the fixpoint, the verdict. `[sim]`
-- is the failure simulator's journal — what actually happened in a run,
-- written by the runtime and by nobody else. It passes the litmus of
-- docs/choosing-perspectives.md without argument: the simulator and the
-- fixpoint can legitimately disagree about one state and neither entry is an
-- error (that disagreement is the whole of §11); the book is not computed
-- from other facts, it is observed; it says whose entry this is, not how the
-- entry was obtained; and its writer is nameable and single —
-- `authority(sim, simulator)`.
--
-- What would be WRONG, and it is the obvious first draft:
-- `outcome(simulator, S, capped)` with the source as an argument column.
-- That loses `forged` — anybody could file a run under the simulator's name
-- — and it turns the crossing between the two books into an invisible join
-- instead of the declared `imports(main, sim)` that `leak[audit]` checks.
--
-- WHERE THE ANALYSIS IS ONLY AS GOOD AS THE MODEL. The state machine here is
-- synthetic and the transitions are written by hand; extracting a machine
-- from real code or a real config is the generative step, and the kernel does
-- not do it. That is the same boundary JOPA meets translating a law and NPC
-- meets generalising a hole: THE KERNEL GUARANTEES CONSEQUENCES, NOT
-- TRANSLATION. It is softer here than anywhere else in the corpus, because
-- the translation is checkable — demo.ts §10 re-derives the whole machine in
-- plain TypeScript and compares it transition by transition, and §11 runs it.

-- =========================================================================
-- 0. the two books
-- =========================================================================

authority(main, rip).
authority(sim, simulator).

-- [main] reads the simulator's journal to compute where the two disagree.
-- Both ends are registered ledgers, so this is exactly what `imports` is
-- for; `collects` would be the wrong sentence, and a false one — [main] here
-- names the book it gathers from.
imports(main, sim).

-- The same crossing the other way, which the leak audit asks about now that
-- the flow graph is transitively closed: the journal reaches [main] through
-- `crosscheck/3`, every audit rule reads [main], so the journal reaches
-- [audit]. Licensed on purpose, and not a formality — `forged[audit]` exists
-- precisely to say that somebody other than the simulator filed a run, and
-- it cannot say so without reading the journal. demo.ts §11 files one under
-- the wrong name and watches the row appear.
imports(audit, sim).

-- The journal is an INPUT: the runtime writes it, no rule concludes it, and
-- an empty journal is a legitimate state of the world (the model answers
-- perfectly well before any simulation has run). Declaring an input is the
-- remedy for `undefined_premise`; a waiver is not.
edb(sim_settled).
edb(sim_failed).
edb(sim_capped).
edb(sim_seen).

-- =========================================================================
-- 1. THE POLICY — the retry rules, and the whole switch
-- =========================================================================
-- Everything in this block is a decision somebody made about how the
-- workflow behaves. demo.ts §9 substitutes this block, one line at a time,
-- and measures what each line is worth in states rescued or lost. Nothing
-- below the @machine marker mentions any of these numbers.
--
-- @policy

-- How many times a stage may be attempted. `limit(S, N)` allows a call at
-- attempt 0..N, so N+1 attempts. The compensations get a bigger budget than
-- the forward path, which is what real workflows do and what this analysis
-- shows is load-bearing: with `limit(refund, 1)` the environment can burn
-- both refund attempts inside its own slack and the compensation stops being
-- forceable. MEASURED, on this machine at slack 2: 12 states lose their
-- guarantee, ten of them inside the refund itself, and the initial state is
-- NOT among them — it keeps a route home through a charge that never lands.
-- Which is the point of counting rather than arguing.
limit(reserve, 1).
limit(charge, 1).
limit(ship, 1).
limit(refund, 2).
limit(release, 2).

-- Which stages can ask the provider what really happened. This is the one
-- defence against a non-idempotent retry, and demo.ts §9 measures it.
probe_of(charge, verify).
probe_of(ship, track).

-- Where a stage goes when the workflow stops trying and starts undoing.
aborts_to(reserve, release).
aborts_to(charge, refund).
aborts_to(ship, refund).

-- THE POLICY LINE THAT COSTS THE MOST, and it is not the retry count.
-- "Only compensate when you know where you stand" is a sensible-sounding
-- rule and it strands the reserve stage: a reserve that timed out leaves the
-- workflow unsure, unable to abort, and with one attempt left. Reserving is
-- IDEMPOTENT, so the doubt is harmless and the rule is unnecessary there —
-- but nothing in the policy says which stages are idempotent, so the rule
-- applies to all of them. demo.ts §9 deletes the reserve line and counts.
abort_needs_certainty(reserve).
abort_needs_certainty(charge).
abort_needs_certainty(ship).

-- THE FAIRNESS ASSUMPTION, stated as a number because it has to be.
--
-- An environment that may misbehave for ever defeats every workflow: it
-- answers `fail` to every attempt of every stage including the compensation,
-- and then NOTHING is guaranteed and the analysis says "no strategy exists"
-- about a perfectly healthy machine. The assumption real reliability work
-- makes instead is that providers eventually behave; the assumption a finite
-- AND/OR fixpoint can express is that they misbehave at most `slack` times
-- over the whole run.
--
-- It is a strictly weaker statement than fairness and it is the honest one:
-- every verdict below is relative to this number, and demo.ts §9 sweeps it
-- from 0 to 3 so that the sensitivity is visible rather than assumed. Note
-- that `silence` costs the environment NOTHING — silence is not an attempt
-- that failed, it is an attempt that never came back — which is why an
-- unbounded play survives a bounded misbehaviour budget, and why the third
-- category exists at all.
slack(2).

-- =========================================================================
-- 2. THE MACHINE
-- =========================================================================
-- @machine

-- A state is `w(At, T, D, R, C, Sh, F)`:
--
--   At   where the workflow is: the stage it is about to call, or a terminal
--   T    attempts already spent at this stage
--   D    doubt — 1 if the last answer left the workflow unsure what landed
--   R    a reservation is held
--   C    how many times money has actually moved
--   Sh   how many parcels have actually been dispatched
--   F    how much misbehaviour the environment has left (the slack above)
--
-- WHAT IS HIDDEN FROM THE WORKFLOW AND WHAT IS NOT. `At`, `T` and `D` are
-- the workflow's own bookkeeping and it can read them. `R`, `C` and `Sh` are
-- the WORLD, and the workflow learns them only by being told — which is what
-- a probe is for and why a lost answer is not the same as a failure. Two
-- states differing only in `C` look identical from inside the workflow. That
-- gap is real and it is measured: demo.ts §12 checks whether the strategy the
-- fixpoint certifies ever needs to distinguish two states the workflow
-- cannot tell apart, and names the classes where it does.

stage(reserve). stage(charge). stage(ship).
stage(verify). stage(track). stage(refund). stage(release).

terminal(fulfilled). terminal(cancelled). terminal(abandoned).

-- Which external system each stage talks to. Reporting only — no rule turns
-- on it — and it is here because "which provider carries the risk" is the
-- first question anybody asks of the answer.
provider(reserve, inventory).
provider(release, inventory).
provider(charge, payments).
provider(refund, payments).
provider(verify, payments).
provider(ship, carrier).
provider(track, carrier).

-- WHICH STAGES ARE IDEMPOTENT, and this is the core of the domain rather
-- than an annotation. Reserving twice with the same token holds one
-- reservation; refunding an order twice refunds it once; releasing twice
-- releases once. CHARGING twice charges twice, and DISPATCHING twice sends
-- two parcels. Reporting only, again — the difference is written into the
-- transitions of §2.3, where an idempotent stage's `ok` does not depend on
-- what already landed and a non-idempotent one's does.
idempotent(reserve).
idempotent(release).
idempotent(refund).

-- -------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2.1 the environment's repertoire
--
-- Five behaviours, and each one has to leave a DIFFERENT mark or it is a
-- fifth name for a third behaviour:
--
--   ok        the effect applied and the workflow was told
--   fail      a definite no. Nothing applied, and the workflow knows it
--   timeout   no answer inside the deadline. Nothing applied, and the
--             workflow does NOT know that — it only knows it did not hear
--   lost      the effect applied and the answer did not come back. The
--             money moved; the workflow is retrying. This is the one the
--             whole example is about
--   silence   nothing came back at all, and nothing ever will. The retry
--             timer never fires, no attempt is consumed, the task simply
--             waits. Only a push-based provider can do this
--
-- `fail` and `timeout` differ in the workflow's belief (D), `timeout` and
-- `lost` differ in the world (C, Sh) and are INDISTINGUISHABLE from inside
-- the workflow, and `silence` differs from all of them by consuming nothing.

free(ok).
free(silence).
costly(fail).
costly(timeout).
costly(lost).

-- The repertoire is per stage rather than per provider, because a read has
-- no effect to lose: `verify` and `track` ask a question and cannot answer
-- `lost`. Only the carrier can go quiet — a payment gateway is
-- request/response behind a hard deadline, so it produces a timeout where
-- the carrier produces nothing at all. That asymmetry is a claim about the
-- world, it is the sole source of the third category in this machine, and
-- demo.ts §5 tests it from both directions.
answers(reserve, ok).
answers(reserve, fail).
answers(reserve, timeout).
answers(reserve, lost).
answers(release, ok).
answers(release, fail).
answers(release, timeout).
answers(release, lost).
answers(charge, ok).
answers(charge, fail).
answers(charge, timeout).
answers(charge, lost).
answers(refund, ok).
answers(refund, fail).
answers(refund, timeout).
answers(refund, lost).
answers(verify, ok).
answers(verify, fail).
answers(verify, timeout).
answers(ship, ok).
answers(ship, fail).
answers(ship, timeout).
answers(ship, lost).
answers(ship, silence).
answers(track, ok).
answers(track, fail).
answers(track, timeout).
answers(track, silence).

-- -------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2.2 the reachable state space
--
-- `reached` carries the move that got there IN ITS SUPPORT, which is what
-- makes a shortest-path fold over it readable back as a script of failures.
-- `state` is the same set without the reading; §8 of demo.ts walks the one
-- and §6 explains from the other.

initial(w(reserve, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, F)) :- slack(F).

reached(S)  :- initial(S).
reached(S2) :- reached(S), respond(S, _, _, S2).
state(S)    :- reached(S).

-- -------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2.3 what the workflow may do, and what the environment may answer
--
-- The workflow's alphabet is four moves. `call` makes the stage's external
-- call. `abort` stops trying and starts undoing. `check` asks the provider
-- what really happened. `park` gives up: the task goes to the dead letter
-- queue, which is a terminal and is never settled, so parking is never a
-- solution — it is available at every stage and the strategy never picks it,
-- which is the point of having it in the model at all.

action(w(At,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call) :- state(w(At,T,D,R,C,Sh,F)), limit(At, N), T <= N.
action(w(At,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call) :- state(w(At,T,D,R,C,Sh,F)), probe_of(_, At).

action(w(At,T,0,R,C,Sh,F), abort) :- state(w(At,T,0,R,C,Sh,F)), aborts_to(At, _).
action(w(At,T,1,R,C,Sh,F), abort) :- state(w(At,T,1,R,C,Sh,F)), aborts_to(At, _),
                                     not abort_needs_certainty(At).

action(w(At,T,1,R,C,Sh,F), check) :- state(w(At,T,1,R,C,Sh,F)), probe_of(At, _).

action(w(At,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), park) :- state(w(At,T,D,R,C,Sh,F)), stage(At).

-- What the environment may answer to a call at this state: anything in the
-- stage's repertoire, and a costly answer only while it has slack left.
may(w(At,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), A) :- action(w(At,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call), answers(At, A), free(A).
may(w(At,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), A) :- action(w(At,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call), answers(At, A), costly(A), F >= 1.

-- The three internal moves. `none` is the answer of a move that talks to
-- nobody, and it keeps `respond/4` one relation instead of two.
respond(w(At,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), park, none, w(abandoned,0,0,R,C,Sh,F)) :-
    action(w(At,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), park).
respond(w(At,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), abort, none, w(To,0,D,R,C,Sh,F)) :-
    action(w(At,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), abort), aborts_to(At, To).
respond(w(At,T,1,R,C,Sh,F), check, none, w(Pr,T,1,R,C,Sh,F)) :-
    action(w(At,T,1,R,C,Sh,F), check), probe_of(At, Pr).

-- ---- reserve: IDEMPOTENT. `ok` holds one reservation whatever was held
-- before, so a lost answer costs the workflow an attempt and nothing else.
respond(w(reserve,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, ok, w(charge,0,0,1,C,Sh,F)) :-
    may(w(reserve,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), ok).
respond(w(reserve,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, fail, w(reserve,T1,D,R,C,Sh,F1)) :-
    may(w(reserve,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), fail), T1 is T + 1, F1 is F - 1.
respond(w(reserve,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, timeout, w(reserve,T1,1,R,C,Sh,F1)) :-
    may(w(reserve,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), timeout), T1 is T + 1, F1 is F - 1.
respond(w(reserve,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, lost, w(reserve,T1,1,1,C,Sh,F1)) :-
    may(w(reserve,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), lost), T1 is T + 1, F1 is F - 1.

-- ---- charge: NOT IDEMPOTENT. Both `ok` and `lost` move money, and `lost`
-- moves it without telling anyone. The retry is not an edge back into the
-- same state; it is an edge into a NEW one with the side effect accumulated,
-- and that is where the adversarial structure comes from.
respond(w(charge,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, ok, w(ship,0,0,R,C1,Sh,F)) :-
    may(w(charge,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), ok), C < 2, C1 is C + 1.
respond(w(charge,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, fail, w(charge,T1,D,R,C,Sh,F1)) :-
    may(w(charge,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), fail), T1 is T + 1, F1 is F - 1.
respond(w(charge,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, timeout, w(charge,T1,1,R,C,Sh,F1)) :-
    may(w(charge,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), timeout), T1 is T + 1, F1 is F - 1.
respond(w(charge,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, lost, w(charge,T1,1,R,C1,Sh,F1)) :-
    may(w(charge,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), lost), C < 2, C1 is C + 1, T1 is T + 1, F1 is F - 1.

-- ---- verify: one shot, charged against the SAME attempt budget as the
-- stage it is protecting, because a probe that had a budget of its own would
-- let the workflow ask for ever. Its `ok` reveals the world, and the three
-- rules below are the three things it can find.
respond(w(verify,T,D,R,0,Sh,F), call, ok, w(charge,T,0,R,0,Sh,F)) :-
    may(w(verify,T,D,R,0,Sh,F), ok).
respond(w(verify,T,D,R,1,Sh,F), call, ok, w(ship,0,0,R,1,Sh,F)) :-
    may(w(verify,T,D,R,1,Sh,F), ok).
respond(w(verify,T,D,R,2,Sh,F), call, ok, w(refund,0,0,R,2,Sh,F)) :-
    may(w(verify,T,D,R,2,Sh,F), ok).
respond(w(verify,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, fail, w(charge,T1,1,R,C,Sh,F1)) :-
    may(w(verify,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), fail), T1 is T + 1, F1 is F - 1.
respond(w(verify,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, timeout, w(charge,T1,1,R,C,Sh,F1)) :-
    may(w(verify,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), timeout), T1 is T + 1, F1 is F - 1.

-- ---- ship: NOT IDEMPOTENT, and the only stage that can go quiet. `ok`
-- consumes the reservation and finishes; `lost` sends a parcel nobody
-- acknowledged; `silence` changes nothing but the doubt and costs the
-- environment nothing, which is the self-loop the third category is made of.
respond(w(ship,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, ok, w(fulfilled,0,0,0,C,Sh1,F)) :-
    may(w(ship,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), ok), Sh < 2, Sh1 is Sh + 1.
respond(w(ship,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, fail, w(ship,T1,D,R,C,Sh,F1)) :-
    may(w(ship,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), fail), T1 is T + 1, F1 is F - 1.
respond(w(ship,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, timeout, w(ship,T1,1,R,C,Sh,F1)) :-
    may(w(ship,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), timeout), T1 is T + 1, F1 is F - 1.
respond(w(ship,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, lost, w(ship,T1,1,R,C,Sh1,F1)) :-
    may(w(ship,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), lost), Sh < 2, Sh1 is Sh + 1, T1 is T + 1, F1 is F - 1.
respond(w(ship,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, silence, w(ship,T,1,R,C,Sh,F)) :-
    may(w(ship,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), silence).

-- ---- track: the carrier's probe, and it inherits the carrier's silence.
-- That is why the fix that rescues the charge stage does not rescue this
-- one: asking a provider that has gone quiet is itself quiet.
respond(w(track,T,D,R,C,0,F), call, ok, w(ship,T,0,R,C,0,F)) :-
    may(w(track,T,D,R,C,0,F), ok).
respond(w(track,T,D,R,C,1,F), call, ok, w(fulfilled,0,0,0,C,1,F)) :-
    may(w(track,T,D,R,C,1,F), ok).
respond(w(track,T,D,R,C,2,F), call, ok, w(refund,0,0,R,C,2,F)) :-
    may(w(track,T,D,R,C,2,F), ok).
respond(w(track,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, fail, w(ship,T1,1,R,C,Sh,F1)) :-
    may(w(track,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), fail), T1 is T + 1, F1 is F - 1.
respond(w(track,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, timeout, w(ship,T1,1,R,C,Sh,F1)) :-
    may(w(track,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), timeout), T1 is T + 1, F1 is F - 1.
respond(w(track,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, silence, w(track,T,1,R,C,Sh,F)) :-
    may(w(track,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), silence).

-- ---- refund: IDEMPOTENT, and it refunds the order rather than a charge, so
-- `ok` from a double charge still lands on zero. A compensation that can
-- itself fail is the whole reason the compensation budget is larger than the
-- forward one.
respond(w(refund,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, ok, w(release,0,0,R,0,Sh,F)) :-
    may(w(refund,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), ok).
respond(w(refund,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, fail, w(refund,T1,D,R,C,Sh,F1)) :-
    may(w(refund,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), fail), T1 is T + 1, F1 is F - 1.
respond(w(refund,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, timeout, w(refund,T1,1,R,C,Sh,F1)) :-
    may(w(refund,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), timeout), T1 is T + 1, F1 is F - 1.
respond(w(refund,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, lost, w(refund,T1,1,R,0,Sh,F1)) :-
    may(w(refund,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), lost), T1 is T + 1, F1 is F - 1.

-- ---- release: IDEMPOTENT, and the last thing that happens on the way out.
respond(w(release,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, ok, w(cancelled,0,0,0,C,Sh,F)) :-
    may(w(release,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), ok).
respond(w(release,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, fail, w(release,T1,D,R,C,Sh,F1)) :-
    may(w(release,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), fail), T1 is T + 1, F1 is F - 1.
respond(w(release,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, timeout, w(release,T1,1,R,C,Sh,F1)) :-
    may(w(release,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), timeout), T1 is T + 1, F1 is F - 1.
respond(w(release,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), call, lost, w(release,T1,1,0,C,Sh,F1)) :-
    may(w(release,T,D,R,C,Sh,F), lost), T1 is T + 1, F1 is F - 1.

-- -------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2.4 when the books balance
--
-- A terminal is SETTLED when the world is consistent, and consistency is a
-- statement about money and goods, not about the workflow's feelings. There
-- are exactly two consistent endings: the order was fulfilled — charged
-- once, dispatched once, reservation consumed — or it was cancelled with
-- nothing charged, nothing sent and nothing held.
--
-- `abandoned` is never settled, whatever the world looks like. That is what
-- a dead letter queue IS: the task stopped without an answer. It is also the
-- line that keeps the analysis honest, because a `park` that counted as
-- settled whenever the world happened to be clean would let the fixpoint
-- certify the strategy "give up at exactly the right moment", which no
-- workflow can implement.

settled(w(fulfilled,T,D,0,1,1,F)) :- state(w(fulfilled,T,D,0,1,1,F)).
settled(w(cancelled,T,D,0,0,0,F)) :- state(w(cancelled,T,D,0,0,0,F)).

-- -------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2.5 the machine's own audit
--
-- An action with no responses would be VACUOUSLY safe — `risky` needs a
-- response to fire, so an action nobody can answer makes `will_settle` true
-- for free. That is a soundness hole in the model rather than in the kernel,
-- and it is exactly the shape of hole a cap on an arithmetic argument opens:
-- raise `limit(charge, 2)` and a third charge has no `ok` successor, because
-- `C < 2` refuses it. This relation is the gate that says so. It is empty in
-- the baseline and demo.ts §2 makes it non-empty on purpose.

has_response(S, A) :- respond(S, A, _, _).
dangling(S, A) :- action(S, A), not has_response(S, A).

-- =========================================================================
-- 3. THE GAME
-- =========================================================================
-- Everything below this marker is the alternating fixpoint and what reads
-- it. demo.ts §3 loads this half on its own against the machine of §2 dumped
-- as facts, and checks that the staged answer and the whole-file answer
-- agree state for state.
--
-- @game

semantics(well_founded).

-- THE ONE PLACE THE QUANTIFIERS ALTERNATE.
--
--   will_settle(S)  there EXISTS an action all of whose answers settle
--   risky(S, A)     there EXISTS an answer to A that does not settle
--
-- `not risky(S, A)` is the universal quantifier: for ALL answers, the
-- successor settles. Written directly it would be a universal over an open
-- set, which no rule language has; written this way it is two existentials
-- and one negation, and the negation is what puts the pair in a cycle.
--
-- Read the pair as a game and both halves are ordinary. The workflow moves
-- first and wants to reach a settled terminal; the environment answers and
-- wants to stop it. What the alternating fixpoint computes is the standard
-- three-way split of such a game: the workflow's winning region, the
-- environment's winning region, and the states where neither can force its
-- own end and the play may go on for ever.

will_settle(S) :- settled(S).
will_settle(S) :- action(S, A), not risky(S, A).
risky(S, A)    :- respond(S, A, _, S2), not will_settle(S2).

-- The other two categories, and they are two different kinds of sentence.
--
-- `doomed` is DERIVED: it is the environment's winning region, and it is
-- true of a state exactly where the alternation makes `will_settle` false —
-- no round derives it, however generously negation is judged. Note what it
-- does NOT mean: not "no lucky sequence exists". A doomed state can still be
-- reached by a run that happens to finish (§6 counts them). It means the
-- environment has a strategy that stops it, so retrying is not the fix.
--
-- `dead_letter` READS THE THIRD VALUE. `unknown/1` is a value like any
-- other here: a rule may match it, and this one does. Its rows are the
-- complement of the fixpoint, they carry the firing that derived them, and
-- they are the population of the dead letter queue.

doomed(S)      :- state(S), not will_settle(S).
dead_letter(S) :- state(S), unknown(will_settle(S)).

-- One relation carrying all three, so that a report can join on it. The
-- third row is the one that could not be written in a two-valued engine.
verdict(S, settles)     :- will_settle(S).
verdict(S, doomed)      :- doomed(S).
verdict(S, dead_letter) :- dead_letter(S).

-- -------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 3.1 fragility, as a count of plays rather than of states
--
-- `can_settle(S)` is true where SOME play from S ends with the books
-- balanced — luck, not strategy. Folded with the counting semiring it says
-- how many such plays there are, and one is the number that matters: a task
-- with exactly one route home dies the day that route stops existing, and no
-- test fails when it does. Where a silence loop is on the way the count is
-- infinitely many, which is the honest answer and is checked as a
-- divergence rather than assumed — demo.ts §7 refuses to close the cycle and
-- watches the bounded counts grow.

can_settle(S) :- settled(S).
can_settle(S) :- respond(S, _, _, S2), can_settle(S2).

-- -------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 3.2 the cross-check against the simulator's journal
--
-- The simulator writes `sim_seen[sim](Env, S)` for a state it started runs
-- from, and one of `sim_settled`, `sim_failed`, `sim_capped` for each
-- outcome it saw. `capped` is a run that hit the step cap: the simulator's
-- own budget hole, kept apart from both verdicts for the same reason the
-- kernel keeps `hole(Id, budget_exhausted)` apart from `unknown` — a run
-- that was stopped is not a run that finished badly.
--
-- The four outcomes below are named, one relation, one atom each — three
-- disagreements and one agreement, because a comparison that can only report
-- disagreement is not a comparison. A table of rates would say the two agree
-- 94% of the time and destroy exactly what the comparison is for.

-- The certified strategy is not the deployed policy: the model says a
-- strategy exists and the policy in the simulator hangs anyway.
crosscheck(S, Env, policy_hangs_where_a_strategy_exists) :-
    verdict(S, settles), sim_capped[sim](Env, S).

-- The model says the environment can force failure, and under this
-- environment every single run failed — agreement, kept as its own row so
-- the interesting rows can be read against something.
crosscheck(S, Env, agrees_forced_and_observed) :-
    verdict(S, doomed), sim_failed[sim](Env, S), not sim_settled[sim](Env, S).

-- The model says the environment can force failure and this environment
-- never managed it. Not a defect: `doomed` is about what an adversary CAN
-- do, and a random provider is not an adversary.
crosscheck(S, Env, forced_but_never_seen) :-
    verdict(S, doomed), sim_seen[sim](Env, S), not sim_failed[sim](Env, S).

-- The static analysis found a pathology the runs did not reach. This is the
-- row that justifies doing the analysis at all, and it is expected to be
-- large under a random environment and empty under an adversarial one.
crosscheck(S, Env, dead_letter_never_observed) :-
    verdict(S, dead_letter), sim_seen[sim](Env, S), not sim_capped[sim](Env, S).

-- Acceptance scenario (examples/rip/demo.ts runs all of it):
--   ? unstratified(X)              -> will_settle, reported as information
--   ? leak[audit](A, B)            -> empty  (planted leak: one row)
--   ? dangling(S, A)               -> empty  (raise limit(charge,2): non-empty)
--   ? verdict(S, C)                -> 133 states, 77 / 32 / 24
--   ? dead_letter(S)               -> 24, every one of them at ship or track
--   why unknown(will_settle(...))  -> the failure sequence, closing on [cycle]
--   counting can_settle(S)         -> 1 is fragile, infinitely many is a loop
--   ? crosscheck(S, Env, What)     -> named, never averaged
